cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  ctsegdose-core: patient-specific absorbed organ dose from CT tube-current
  modulation, using deep-learning organ segmentation and HU-derived density
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it, and cite ctdose-core for the dose
  engine it builds on.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Yamamoto
    given-names: Shuji
    email: yamamoto@lisit.jp
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9211-1071'
    affiliation: Institute of One, LISIT Co., Ltd., Tokyo, Japan
repository-code: https://github.com/Institute-of-One/ctsegdose-core
url: https://github.com/Institute-of-One/ctsegdose-core
abstract: >-
  A Python package that takes a CT acquisition from its recorded tube-current
  modulation to an absorbed organ dose in mGy, made patient-specific by the
  patient's own anatomy: organs are segmented from the same series with a
  deep-learning segmenter, organ mass is derived from the measured Hounsfield
  units, and the organ-specific weighted CTDIvol of ctdose-core is converted to
  absorbed dose with CTDIvol-normalised organ-dose coefficients scaled to that
  patient. Every value carries its formula, its inputs, its assumptions and its
  source. No imaging is redistributed: the public series used are identified by
  Series Instance UID, collection and licence so they can be re-fetched.
keywords:
  - computed tomography
  - organ dose
  - absorbed dose
  - tube current modulation
  - organ segmentation
  - Hounsfield units
  - DICOM
  - reproducibility
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
references:
  - type: software
    title: >-
      ctdose-core: open, auditable CT dose surveillance from DICOM, with a
      physics reconstruction when the dose attributes are missing
    doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21636082
    notes: >-
      The open dose engine this work builds on. It stops at the dose index; this
      package continues to absorbed organ dose.
    authors:
      - family-names: Yamamoto
        given-names: Shuji
        orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9211-1071'
        affiliation: Institute of One, LISIT Co., Ltd., Tokyo, Japan
